# jxl-encoder NEON vs forced-scalar — first tier-isolated measurement
date:    2026-07-28
host:    Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS, aarch64
command: cargo bench -p jxl-encoder --bench tier_isolation --features _dev
build:   release, NO -C target-cpu=native
input:   512x512 noise+patches (a gradient would give degenerate DCT
         coefficients and understate the transform kernels), effort 5

## Result — the split is by coding mode

| mode                  | NEON     | scalar   | speedup |
|-----------------------|----------|----------|---------|
| lossy d1.0 (VarDCT)   |  54.86ms | 115.40ms |  2.10x  |
| lossless (modular)    | 240.27ms | 283.39ms |  1.18x  |

No regression.

VarDCT more than doubles: that path runs the XYB transform, DCT8/DCT64,
Gaborish 5x5, EPF and chroma-from-luma kernels, all of them
`#[magetypes(... neon ...)]` in jxl-encoder-simd. Modular gains far less
because it is predictor + entropy coded, which is serial.

This independently reproduces the same split measured on the DECODER side the
same day (zenjxl-decoder: VarDCT 1.71-1.83x, modular 1.02-1.05x). Two separate
codebases, same conclusion — the mode, not the implementation, is what decides
how much NEON is worth in JPEG XL.

## Why this bench had to be added

The existing benches here (`dedup_samples_strategies`,
`predictor_prune_microbench`, `tree_learn_split_read_pattern`) are microbenches
of specific modular tree-learning strategies. None compares the encoder against
itself with SIMD disabled, so nothing measured what the SIMD crate is worth —
on any architecture.

NOT MEASURED: per-kernel attribution, and only one effort level / one distance.
